.TH PREBUILD 1 "July 14, 1992" "NeXT Computer, Inc."
.SH NAME
prebuild \- Build PostScript prebuilt font file from bitmap description files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /usr/bin/prebuild
.I fontname
.I fonttype
.I fontuniqueid
.I fontcharactersetname
.I bdffile ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B prebuild
is used to build a binary file format known as the prebuilt
file for use by the NEXTSTEP(tm) Window Server.
It takes as input a number of standard
Adobe Bitmap Description format files and
produces a much more compact binary representation
of the bits in those files.  The Bitmap Distribution
Format files provided as input to
.B prebuild
must be given in order from smallest to largest.
.PP
In Display PostScript, every face of every font is defined
by a PostScript program (see the PostScript\*R Language
Reference Manual for more details on how to create a 
PostScript font).  In order for the NEXTSTEP system to
use a font, the font creator must provide the PostScript
language file defining the outlines of the characters
of the font, and an Adobe Font Metrics (AFM) file describing
separately the widths of those characters.
.PP
In NEXTSTEP a font is stored in the filesystem as a directory
whose name is that of the font, followed by a \fB.font\fR extension;
for example,
.nh
\fBHelvetica-Oblique.font\fR.
.hy
The \fB.font\fR directory must contain at least the outline and afm
files for the font, and optionally the bitmap file for the font.
So,
.nh
Helvetica-Oblique.font
.hy
would contain the outline file
.nh
\fBHelvetica-Oblique\fR,
.hy
the AFM file
.nh
\fBHelvetica-Oblique.afm\fR,
.hy
and possibly
.nh
\fBHelvetica-Oblique.bepf\fR
.hy
(see below).
Some fonts also have AFM files for specific point sizes of screen
bitmap fonts; for example,
.nh
\fBScreen-Helvetica-Oblique.10.afm\fR
.hy
for 10-point Helvetica Oblique.
.PP
In order for the window server to see a new font,
it must be placed in one of the three standard Fonts directories:
\fB/NextLibrary/Fonts\fR,
\fB/LocalLibrary/Fonts\fR,
or
\fB~/Library/Fonts\fR.
.PP
.B prebuild
outputs a file in the current directory whose name is the name
of the font followed by the suffix indicating the byte sex of
the file
(\fB.bepf\fR for big-endian files,
and \fB.lepf\fR for little-endian files).
This file should be placed in the same font's \fB.font\fR directory.
.LP
.PP
.SH FILES
.ta \w'/LocalLibrary/Fonts/    'u
.nf
/NextLibrary/Fonts/	location of NeXT-provided fonts
/LocalLibrary/Fonts/	location of local fonts
~/Library/Fonts/	location of user fonts
.fi
.DT
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
screenafm(1)
.SH BUGS
.B prebuild
should output both a \fB.bepf\fR and a \fB.lepf\fR file.  
.PP
\fIfonttype\fR and \fIfontuniqueid\fR are actually ignored.
